WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.380 --> 00:00:05.540
Hello.

00:00:05.580 --> 00:00:07.900
Let me start by showing you
a construction site

00:00:07.940 --> 00:00:10.060
in my home city of Oslo.

00:00:10.700 --> 00:00:12.940
(Sounds of children playing)

00:00:28.300 --> 00:00:30.500
Did you hear something unusual?

00:00:30.500 --> 00:00:32.460
Indeed, the noise.

00:00:32.500 --> 00:00:35.220
The noise you hear
was from the kindergarten nearby,

00:00:35.260 --> 00:00:36.940
not from the machine noise.

00:00:36.980 --> 00:00:38.500
The machines were electric,

00:00:38.540 --> 00:00:43.420
and so were the trucks that transport
materials to and from the site.

00:00:43.460 --> 00:00:46.420
As head of the climate agency in Oslo,

00:00:46.460 --> 00:00:50.500
the Klosterenga construction
site is a climate measure

00:00:50.540 --> 00:00:52.980
I'm really excited about.

00:00:53.580 --> 00:00:56.700
The workers here are opening a stream

00:00:56.740 --> 00:01:01.500
that has been confined in pipelines
for more than 120 years.

00:01:02.180 --> 00:01:05.900
And as climate changes in Oslo,

00:01:05.940 --> 00:01:09.460
we are likely to experience
more heavy rain.

00:01:09.460 --> 00:01:13.980
So we need to improve
our storm water runoff system,

00:01:14.020 --> 00:01:16.580
and opening creeks and rivers

00:01:16.620 --> 00:01:18.980
will help the city
to become more resilient

00:01:19.020 --> 00:01:20.340
and avoid flooding,

00:01:20.380 --> 00:01:23.420
as well as creating nicer areas

00:01:23.420 --> 00:01:27.460
and contributing
to biodiversity in the city.

00:01:28.020 --> 00:01:32.580
Klosterenga is close to being
a zero-emission construction site

00:01:32.620 --> 00:01:34.700
run by the city of Oslo.

00:01:34.740 --> 00:01:40.900
And running zero-emission sites is just
one example of the Oslo climate strategy.

00:01:41.340 --> 00:01:47.140
This is how downtown of Oslo looked
when I moved to Oslo in 1989.

00:01:47.700 --> 00:01:50.100
This is the same area today.

00:01:50.740 --> 00:01:57.020
Oslo's climate measures have given
the city cleaner air, less noise,

00:01:57.060 --> 00:01:59.820
safer and more human-centered streets.

00:02:00.300 --> 00:02:04.180
Some of the measures were controversial
when first introduced,

00:02:04.220 --> 00:02:09.140
but now a majority
of the population thinks --

00:02:09.140 --> 00:02:13.140
even those who are not
that concerned about climate change --

00:02:13.180 --> 00:02:16.020
think that we are creating a better city.

00:02:17.500 --> 00:02:18.660
For too long,

00:02:18.700 --> 00:02:23.060
discussions about climate change
have been about sacrifice.

00:02:23.580 --> 00:02:25.300
I am here to tell you

00:02:25.300 --> 00:02:29.620
that climate solutions
actually make a better city

00:02:29.660 --> 00:02:32.300
and a better quality of life for everyone.

00:02:32.900 --> 00:02:36.380
This is only the first
of five lessons we have learned

00:02:36.380 --> 00:02:39.340
on our journey to become
an emission-free city.

00:02:39.860 --> 00:02:44.220
And I think these lessons
can be applied almost anywhere.

00:02:44.740 --> 00:02:46.660
So I like to share them.

00:02:46.700 --> 00:02:49.380
So lesson number one,

00:02:49.380 --> 00:02:53.180
confronting climate change
is about creating better cities

00:02:53.220 --> 00:02:56.020
and better quality of life for everyone.

00:02:56.540 --> 00:02:58.900
Including those kids in the kindergarten,

00:02:58.940 --> 00:03:02.940
that don't have to share
their days with noisy excavators.

00:03:03.780 --> 00:03:08.100
The second lesson
is about being ambitious.

00:03:08.780 --> 00:03:10.740
In 2015,

00:03:10.740 --> 00:03:15.580
the city council set a target
of reducing greenhouse gas emissions

00:03:15.580 --> 00:03:19.020
by 95 percent by 2030

00:03:19.060 --> 00:03:22.180
without purchasing any carbon offsets.

00:03:22.220 --> 00:03:24.380
So aiming for real reductions.

00:03:24.900 --> 00:03:27.500
Commitment to this goal

00:03:27.540 --> 00:03:29.100
changed our mindset.

00:03:29.540 --> 00:03:31.540
If we were going to reach this target,

00:03:31.580 --> 00:03:34.140
every department had to get active,

00:03:34.180 --> 00:03:36.140
figure out measures

00:03:36.180 --> 00:03:38.900
and finding out how to implement them.

00:03:38.940 --> 00:03:41.740
So second lesson, be ambitious.

00:03:41.780 --> 00:03:45.740
A target measured in years, not decades,

00:03:45.780 --> 00:03:49.300
provides no excuse for inaction.

00:03:51.180 --> 00:03:53.100
In my experience,

00:03:53.140 --> 00:03:56.740
the key challenges
of achieving climate targets

00:03:56.780 --> 00:04:00.500
is that the division of responsibility
we see on global,

00:04:00.540 --> 00:04:02.420
national and local levels.

00:04:02.940 --> 00:04:04.820
In the city, the same is true.

00:04:04.860 --> 00:04:07.740
There are differences
inside the administration,

00:04:07.780 --> 00:04:09.140
between departments

00:04:09.180 --> 00:04:10.780
and between agency,

00:04:10.820 --> 00:04:13.380
which each have their own targets

00:04:13.380 --> 00:04:17.660
and own responsibility
unrelated to climate,

00:04:17.700 --> 00:04:19.820
and their own budget to stick to.

00:04:20.180 --> 00:04:25.860
This makes it very difficult
to coordinate climate measures

00:04:25.900 --> 00:04:29.580
which often cross sectoral boundaries.

00:04:29.580 --> 00:04:33.820
Our a solution to this fragmentation

00:04:33.860 --> 00:04:36.180
was this document:

00:04:36.180 --> 00:04:39.020
the Oslo Climate Budget,

00:04:39.540 --> 00:04:43.580
which we put forward
for the first time in 2016.

00:04:43.620 --> 00:04:46.500
The first climate budget,
as far as we know.

00:04:46.860 --> 00:04:49.460
A climate budget is a governance tool.

00:04:49.460 --> 00:04:54.460
It structures and organizes how we can get
from climate target policies and words

00:04:54.460 --> 00:04:56.620
to action and results.

00:04:56.940 --> 00:05:01.900
It also makes all parts
of the administration in Oslo

00:05:01.940 --> 00:05:04.260
a stakeholder to the climate goals.

00:05:04.820 --> 00:05:06.060
Our climate budget,

00:05:06.100 --> 00:05:10.260
which is measured in tons
of CO2 emissions,

00:05:10.300 --> 00:05:13.620
is fully integrated
in the municipal budget.

00:05:14.380 --> 00:05:16.900
And like the financial budget,

00:05:16.940 --> 00:05:23.060
the climate budget is the responsibility
of the deputy mayor of finance.

00:05:23.820 --> 00:05:27.020
It presents the expected
emission reduction

00:05:27.060 --> 00:05:30.060
from the adopted climate measures.

00:05:30.100 --> 00:05:33.820
And it clearly states what has to be done

00:05:33.860 --> 00:05:35.700
by whom, when,

00:05:35.700 --> 00:05:39.900
and, where relevant, at what
necessary budgetary cost.

00:05:40.580 --> 00:05:43.700
Let me give you one example.

00:05:43.740 --> 00:05:46.020
In 2022,

00:05:46.060 --> 00:05:51.740
the city council decided to invest
in a carbon capture and storage facility

00:05:51.780 --> 00:05:55.460
at the largest waste
incineration plant in Oslo.

00:05:55.500 --> 00:06:00.140
This is the single biggest
emission source in Oslo,

00:06:00.180 --> 00:06:03.820
accounting for 17 percent of the total.

00:06:04.820 --> 00:06:11.100
This is a measure that has been planned
in the climate budget since 2017,

00:06:11.140 --> 00:06:16.740
and it will be the department of business
development and public ownership,

00:06:16.740 --> 00:06:21.900
with its specific skills, that will have
the responsibility for implementing it.

00:06:21.900 --> 00:06:26.300
And the carbon will be captured
from 2026 and onwards.

00:06:26.860 --> 00:06:30.180
That's the third lesson learned.

00:06:30.220 --> 00:06:36.540
A comprehensive government system
is key to distribute responsibility

00:06:36.580 --> 00:06:40.580
and to keep the responsible
entity accountable.

00:06:41.180 --> 00:06:46.220
An important point is
that every agency will have to report

00:06:46.260 --> 00:06:49.460
on the status of all their climate work

00:06:49.460 --> 00:06:52.220
as a part of the ordinary budget process

00:06:52.220 --> 00:06:54.380
three times a year.

00:06:54.940 --> 00:06:57.900
So let me go back to construction.

00:06:58.340 --> 00:06:59.540
Three years ago,

00:06:59.580 --> 00:07:01.860
we broke ground on the piloting

00:07:01.860 --> 00:07:06.100
of the world’s first
zero-emission construction site.

00:07:06.660 --> 00:07:09.340
The agency of urban environment

00:07:09.380 --> 00:07:14.220
was transforming a busy street

00:07:14.260 --> 00:07:18.780
into a pedestrian zone
in the city center of Oslo.

00:07:19.260 --> 00:07:25.340
They were using powerful machineries
to lift and break slabs of asphalt,

00:07:25.380 --> 00:07:28.380
even though the machinery was so quiet

00:07:28.380 --> 00:07:33.220
that the next door cafes and restaurants
kept their front doors open.

00:07:34.060 --> 00:07:37.660
Today, we have more than 30 such sites,

00:07:38.100 --> 00:07:41.220
even though electric
construction machinery

00:07:41.260 --> 00:07:44.940
barely existed prior to this project.

00:07:44.980 --> 00:07:48.660
This change has been made
through procurement.

00:07:49.220 --> 00:07:51.260
In 2017,

00:07:51.300 --> 00:07:54.060
the city decided to use procurement

00:07:54.100 --> 00:07:58.260
to drive markets
for developing technologies

00:07:58.260 --> 00:08:00.500
we needed to reach the climate goals.

00:08:00.860 --> 00:08:03.020
Like in every city,

00:08:03.020 --> 00:08:05.940
construction is a big emission source,

00:08:05.940 --> 00:08:07.900
and in Oslo,

00:08:07.940 --> 00:08:13.780
the municipality accounts
for approximately 20 percent of everything

00:08:13.820 --> 00:08:15.340
that is being built.

00:08:15.900 --> 00:08:19.620
Our procurement framework awards companies

00:08:19.620 --> 00:08:22.780
who can provide zero-emission services.

00:08:23.540 --> 00:08:25.700
Since 2019,

00:08:26.780 --> 00:08:32.300
all projects by the municipality
have used this as a core tender criterium.

00:08:32.700 --> 00:08:35.220
This is important for contractors

00:08:35.220 --> 00:08:38.660
because it creates a predictable demand

00:08:38.700 --> 00:08:40.940
for zero-emission solutions.

00:08:42.300 --> 00:08:45.940
Other cities in Norway have started
using the same kind of criteria.

00:08:46.500 --> 00:08:52.180
And the result is that the availability
of zero-emission machines

00:08:52.180 --> 00:08:55.060
is increasing every year in Norway.

00:08:55.740 --> 00:08:58.220
So fourth lesson,

00:08:58.260 --> 00:09:02.060
never, ever underestimate
how much you can change

00:09:02.100 --> 00:09:04.580
by being a demanding customer.

00:09:05.140 --> 00:09:10.340
When the key strength of markets
is their adaptability

00:09:10.340 --> 00:09:12.340
and when you play to that strength,

00:09:12.380 --> 00:09:17.020
it is surprising how much
you can change in a short time.

00:09:17.380 --> 00:09:20.340
We plan to gradually
introduce requirements

00:09:20.380 --> 00:09:24.700
for zero emissions in all construction
sites in the city of Oslo,

00:09:24.740 --> 00:09:27.660
both private and public, by 2030.

00:09:28.500 --> 00:09:33.500
In the last years, the city’s quality
of air has improved significantly.

00:09:33.540 --> 00:09:35.180
Two years ago, a columnist wrote

00:09:35.220 --> 00:09:37.940
that the city smells so nice
now in the summer.

00:09:37.980 --> 00:09:40.580
The reasons are reduced traffic

00:09:40.580 --> 00:09:45.820
and the dramatic rise in proportion
of electric cars and buses.

00:09:46.340 --> 00:09:51.140
Sometimes technology development
exceeds our expectations.

00:09:51.500 --> 00:09:55.380
Ten years ago, few would have forecast
the numbers we see today:

00:09:55.860 --> 00:09:59.260
82 percent of all new passenger cars

00:09:59.260 --> 00:10:01.820
and 36 percent of new vans

00:10:01.820 --> 00:10:07.020
have so far in 2022 been zero-emission.

00:10:07.340 --> 00:10:09.020
And, I think, even better:

00:10:09.020 --> 00:10:15.740
30 percent of new heavy vehicles
have so far been biogas or electric.

00:10:16.500 --> 00:10:18.660
This is very good news

00:10:18.660 --> 00:10:22.340
because mobility is the biggest
emission source in the city,

00:10:22.380 --> 00:10:26.780
accounting for approximately
50 percent of our total emissions.

00:10:28.340 --> 00:10:33.940
Transition to electric mobility has been
supported by a wide range of policies.

00:10:34.820 --> 00:10:37.580
These include national tax exemption,

00:10:37.580 --> 00:10:43.180
a local investment in an effective
network of charging points.

00:10:43.820 --> 00:10:47.620
But procurement has played
an important role here, too.

00:10:48.540 --> 00:10:51.220
Everything the municipality buys

00:10:51.220 --> 00:10:55.380
should be delivered
by zero-emission transport.

00:10:55.820 --> 00:10:59.300
This has been and still is
a gradual process,

00:10:59.340 --> 00:11:03.460
but if three providers can offer it,

00:11:03.460 --> 00:11:08.300
zero emission becomes the minimum
criteria in the procurement.

00:11:09.420 --> 00:11:15.060
Everyone probably knows
that Norway is an oil and gas-rich nation

00:11:15.060 --> 00:11:16.300
and many might think

00:11:16.340 --> 00:11:22.100
that this is the reason why we can invest
in climate solutions in Oslo.

00:11:22.540 --> 00:11:28.180
But the reality is that infrastructure
for public transport,

00:11:28.180 --> 00:11:30.620
walking and biking

00:11:30.660 --> 00:11:34.180
[is] financed by a system of toll roads.

00:11:34.220 --> 00:11:36.620
We have a toll ring in Oslo,

00:11:36.660 --> 00:11:38.740
and to go into that zone,

00:11:38.740 --> 00:11:42.900
a non-electrical vehicle
has to pay a high price.

00:11:43.660 --> 00:11:47.700
And that price will increase 40 percent

00:11:47.740 --> 00:11:50.860
January 1, 2024.

00:11:51.380 --> 00:11:54.140
This have not been [an] easy negotiation,

00:11:54.180 --> 00:11:57.220
and taxes are always controversial.

00:11:57.260 --> 00:12:03.700
But this is [an] important incentive
to transition away from fossil fuels.

00:12:04.380 --> 00:12:09.980
Sometimes technology development
changes markets very rapidly.

00:12:10.020 --> 00:12:16.660
Our climate budget had a goal of reaching
zero-emission public transport by 2028.

00:12:17.020 --> 00:12:20.740
Thanks to decreased costs
and advanced technology,

00:12:20.740 --> 00:12:24.980
we are now heading for reaching
that goal five years away.

00:12:25.500 --> 00:12:30.340
(Applause)

00:12:30.980 --> 00:12:36.340
The city's tram and meters
are already electric,

00:12:36.380 --> 00:12:40.020
and nearly all the ferries are electric.

00:12:40.340 --> 00:12:43.540
And by the end of 2023,

00:12:43.580 --> 00:12:47.860
every bus in Oslo
will be electric as well.

00:12:47.900 --> 00:12:51.380
Bus drivers love this development

00:12:51.420 --> 00:12:54.900
because an electric bus
is more pleasant to drive,

00:12:54.940 --> 00:12:59.140
and it creates no noise nor gases.

00:12:59.540 --> 00:13:01.820
So fifth lesson learned,

00:13:01.860 --> 00:13:05.420
don't underestimate
how technology changes markets

00:13:05.460 --> 00:13:08.100
and how fast these changes can happen

00:13:08.140 --> 00:13:10.260
and how you can incentivize them

00:13:10.300 --> 00:13:13.420
by using the right kind of policies.

00:13:14.300 --> 00:13:17.980
Well, we still have a way to go.

00:13:18.020 --> 00:13:20.860
Our latest climate budget analysis

00:13:20.860 --> 00:13:24.060
shows that we are up
for reaching a reduction

00:13:24.060 --> 00:13:29.620
with the adaptable measures
by 62 percent by 2030.

00:13:29.660 --> 00:13:31.140
So we are doing well,

00:13:31.180 --> 00:13:35.500
but we have to do more,
and we need to develop,

00:13:35.500 --> 00:13:38.060
for instance, we need to develop a system

00:13:38.100 --> 00:13:40.580
for integrating indirect emissions.

00:13:40.620 --> 00:13:42.900
emissions produced outside Oslo

00:13:42.940 --> 00:13:46.340
that are related to the activities
and life in the city.

00:13:46.740 --> 00:13:48.820
But we are working on it.

00:13:49.620 --> 00:13:53.740
Let me finish by telling you
how I move around in the city.

00:13:53.740 --> 00:13:55.500
I walk.

00:13:56.460 --> 00:14:00.940
I haven't mentioned two
core aspects of urban mobility yet,

00:14:00.980 --> 00:14:02.540
walking and biking.

00:14:02.540 --> 00:14:05.060
The city began, years ago,

00:14:05.100 --> 00:14:07.500
to develop as a city
for walkers and bikers.

00:14:07.540 --> 00:14:11.540
And that's one of the things
that I really love about the city.

00:14:11.580 --> 00:14:13.380
Thinking of a city this way

00:14:13.380 --> 00:14:16.460
is about thinking how you organize
your everyday life,

00:14:16.500 --> 00:14:18.820
where you can buy your groceries,

00:14:18.820 --> 00:14:20.980
whether you can go to work,

00:14:20.980 --> 00:14:25.020
whether your kids can use their bicycle
to [go to] the sports activities.

00:14:25.060 --> 00:14:27.740
This is about streets,
it's about locations.

00:14:28.140 --> 00:14:32.700
It is about safe cycling paths
and shortcuts for walking.

00:14:32.740 --> 00:14:35.180
And connecting it all
to the rest of the city

00:14:35.220 --> 00:14:37.820
via a clean public transport.

00:14:38.500 --> 00:14:41.980
The latest Norwegian national survey

00:14:42.020 --> 00:14:45.540
showed that 37 percent

00:14:45.540 --> 00:14:49.980
of all daily trips in Oslo
are done by walking.

00:14:50.380 --> 00:14:54.420
So, despite our very long and cold winter,

00:14:54.420 --> 00:14:58.020
we have become a city of walkers.

00:14:58.700 --> 00:15:00.180
And increasingly,

00:15:00.180 --> 00:15:02.900
if you walk past
a construction site in Oslo,

00:15:02.940 --> 00:15:04.980
you will hear no noise.

00:15:04.980 --> 00:15:08.540
Of course, unless there is
a kindergarten [nearby].

00:15:08.820 --> 00:15:09.980
Thank you.

00:15:10.020 --> 00:15:13.860
(Applause)

